<HelpLayout title="Settings & Data" description="Settings overview, backup, restore, and data export in Badger.">
  <h1>Settings & Data</h1>

  <p>Go to <strong>More → Settings</strong> to access all preferences. The <strong>More → Data &amp; backup</strong> shortcut opens the same screen and scrolls straight to the Your data section.</p>

  <p>Settings is grouped by what you are trying to do: <a href={`#logging`}>Logging</a>, the three timer families (<a href={`#rest-timer`}>Rest timer</a>, <a href={`#set-timer`}>Set timer</a>, and <a href={`#workout-timer`}>Workout timer</a>), <a href={`#coaching`}>Coaching</a>, <a href={`#app`}>App</a>, and <a href={`#data`}>Your data</a>.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="logging">Logging</h2>
  <p>General preferences for recording sets.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unit system</strong> - Kilograms or pounds. This is the default for all exercises; individual exercises can override it.</li>
    <li><strong>Default weight increment</strong> - Used by the progressive overload suggestion and stepper buttons when no per-exercise increment is set.</li>
    <li><strong>Track personal records</strong> - Toggle PR detection and badges. Disable if you prefer not to see PR notifications.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-select next set</strong> - When opening an exercise, automatically highlight the next incomplete set.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-advance to next exercise</strong> - After completing or skipping the last set of an exercise, automatically navigate to the next one.</li>
    <li><strong>Warm-up style</strong> - The default style for the warm-up generator: <strong>Percent</strong> (a ramp as a share of your working weight) or <strong>Plates</strong> (evenly spaced, easy-to-load weights). See <a href={`/help/logging/#set-types`}>Warmup sets</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Default warm-up sets</strong> - How many warm-up sets to generate (1-6). Fewer sets means bigger jumps to your working weight. Both warm-up settings can be overridden per exercise on the exercise edit screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rest-timer">Rest timer</h2>
  <p>Preferences for the rest countdown between sets.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Default rest time</strong> - Duration used when no exercise-specific or session override is set (30s-300s).</li>
    <li><strong>Default warmup rest</strong> - Duration for warmup sets (15s-120s).</li>
    <li><strong>Rest timer auto-start</strong> - Automatically starts the rest timer when you log a working set.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest timer sound</strong> - Play a sound when the timer ends. When enabled, choose from several preset sounds (with preview).</li>
    <li><strong>Rest timer vibration</strong> - Vibrate when the timer ends.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="set-timer">Set timer</h2>
  <p>Preferences for the timed set countdown.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Set timer sound</strong> - Play a sound with the notification that fires when a timed set's <a href={`/help/logging/#set-timer`}>countdown</a> ends and Badger could not announce it aloud. A countdown that ends with the app in front is spoken, and on iPhone so is one that ends with the screen locked, as long as the voice below is on. So this toggle governs the notification only and the spoken cues are not affected by turning it off.</li>
    <li><strong>Set timer voice</strong> - The voice that speaks during a timed set: <strong>Female</strong>, <strong>Male</strong>, or <strong>Off</strong>. Tap a choice to hear it before you commit. This is the switch for everything spoken during the countdown, and it is separate from the notification sound above. <strong>Off</strong> stops the talking, not the alert: a set that ends with your phone locked still gets the "Set complete" notification, with its sound if you left the toggle above on.</li>
    <li><strong>Start rest when the timer ends</strong> - Starts the <a href={`/help/logging/#timer-end`}>rest timer</a> the moment a timed set's countdown reaches zero, before you log the set. On by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Log the set when the timer ends</strong> - Also logs the set and marks it done when the countdown reaches zero, the same as tapping its check. Off by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="workout-timer">Workout timer</h2>
  <p>Preferences for the overall workout clock.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Workout timer auto-start</strong> - Automatically starts the elapsed workout timer when you complete your first set.</li>
    <li><strong>Workout timer auto-stop</strong> - Stops the elapsed timer when you complete the last set of the session.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="coaching">Coaching</h2>
  <p>The features that look at your training and suggest something. All of them are optional, and several stay off until you turn them on.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Progressive overload trigger</strong> - How many consecutive sessions with all sets complete before a weight increase is suggested (1-10).</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-progress routine weights</strong> - Offer to write heavier loads you hit back into the routine plan. See <a href={`/help/progression/#auto-progress`}>Auto-progress routine weights</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscle balance warnings</strong> - Toggle the muscle balance warning system on or off. When it's on, a chip row below it lets you mute individual categories you don't want flagged.</li>
    <li><strong>Strength standards</strong> - Turn the untrained-to-elite reference lines on the big barbell lifts on or off. When on, the row below it holds your answer to the one question the standards need (sex), which you can change or withdraw here at any time. See <a href={`/help/progress/#strength-standards`}>Strength standards</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Plateau detection</strong> - Opens the plateau detection screen. See <a href={`/help/progress/#plateau-detection`}>Plateau detection</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Deload</strong> - Opens the deload configuration screen. See <a href={`/help/tools/#deload`}>Deload</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Plateau detection and deload show their current state on the right of the row, for example "On - Balanced" or "Off", so you can see how they are set without opening them.</p>

  <h2 id="app">App</h2>
  <p>How Badger looks, what it notifies you about, and what it does to your phone while you train.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Theme</strong> - Light, Dark, or System (follows your device setting).</li>
    <li><strong>Accent color</strong> - Choose from a preset palette or pick a custom color. The accent applies globally throughout the app.</li>
    <li><strong>Calendar week start</strong> - Monday, Sunday, or Saturday.</li>
    <li><strong>Default history view</strong> - Whether the History tab opens on the list or the calendar.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep screen on</strong> - Prevents your device from sleeping during a workout.</li>
    <li><strong>Haptic feedback</strong> - Vibration on interactions and timer events.</li>
    <li><strong>Workout notification</strong> - Android only. Shows the current exercise and your next set target in the notification bar while a workout is running, so you can check it from the lock screen. See <a href={`/help/logging/#workout-notification`}>Workout notification</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Workout reminders</strong> - A notification on each weekday a routine day is <a href={`/help/routines/#creating`}>scheduled for</a>. <strong>Remind me at</strong> sets the time it arrives. Changes to a routine's schedule take effect straight away.</li>
    <li><strong>End of workout summary</strong> - Choose what the completion sheet shows when you finish a session.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="training-notes">Training notes</h2>
  <p>Under <strong>More → Training notes</strong> is a private page for your own context - goals, injuries, training history, and anything else worth remembering. It's one free-text page; your notes save automatically as you type, so there's no save button. The text stays on your device and is never required.</p>
  <p>Training notes are included in your <a href={`#backup`}>backup</a>, so they carry across to a new device when you restore. They are <strong>not</strong> shared when you export data or share individual routines. (Program-specific notes belong on the routine itself - see <a href={`/help/routines/#routine-notes`}>Routine notes</a>.)</p>
  <p>At the bottom of the page is <strong>Copy for AI</strong>, which bundles these notes together with your program, recent training, records and equipment into one block of text you can paste into any AI - see <a href={`#ai-context`}>Copying your context for an AI</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="data">Your data</h2>
  <p>All the rows below live in the <strong>Your data</strong> section at the bottom of Settings. Tap <strong>More → Data &amp; backup</strong> to jump straight to it.</p>

  <h3 id="backup">Backup</h3>
  <p>Your workout data is stored locally in a SQLite database, and Badger keeps it backed up in three places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>iPhone: iCloud, on by default.</strong> Your own private iCloud database - Badger's developer cannot read it. Turn it off on the Backup screen or in iOS Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Android: on-phone copies, carried by Android's own backup.</strong> Badger keeps five dated copies on your phone. If your phone's own backup is turned on, Android carries those copies to your Google account for you - see <a href={`#android-backup-setting`}>where to check that setting</a> below.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Drive, an optional extra on either platform.</strong> Backs up after every workout and is the way to move a log between an iPhone and an Android phone, since the other two are locked to their own platform.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4 id="cloud-backup">The Backup screen</h4>
  <p>Open it from <strong>More → Settings → Your data → Backup</strong> (the old Cloud backup screen redirects here). It has three sections:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Automatic</strong> - the iCloud switch and its status on iPhone, or the count of on-phone copies on Android, plus a manual <strong>Back up now</strong> for iCloud.</li>
    <li><strong>Restore a copy</strong> - every backup from every destination, newest first, in one list. Tap a copy to confirm and restore. <strong>Undo last restore</strong> appears when a pre-restore snapshot exists, and <strong>From a file…</strong> restores a <code>.workout_backup</code> file you picked yourself.</li>
    <li><strong>Extra</strong> - the Google Drive switch, <strong>Back up equipment photos</strong> (off by default, iCloud and Drive only), a manual Drive backup, and <strong>Save a backup file</strong> for a one-off export you send yourself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Restore from backup</strong> replaces your current database with the copy you picked. <strong>All current data will be overwritten</strong> - back up first if you need to keep it. Badger checks a copy is intact before replacing anything, so a corrupted or incomplete one is rejected and your current data is left untouched.</p>

  <h4 id="android-backup-setting">Android's automatic restore needs Android backup turned on</h4>
  <p>The on-phone copies only reach your Google account, and so only come back on a new or reinstalled phone, if your phone's own backup was on beforehand. Check it under your phone's <strong>Settings → System → Backup</strong> (the exact path varies by phone maker).</p>
  <p>If it was off, restore instead from a Google Drive backup or a saved file - see <a href={`/help/phone-migration/`}>Moving to a new phone</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Back up equipment photos</strong> is a second opt-in on the Backup screen's Extra section, off by default. Leave it off and iCloud and Drive backups hold your training data only; turn it on and photos of your machines, attachments and bars ride along too.</p>
  <p>The cost is larger files. Photos already on your equipment are included starting with the next backup. The on-phone copies Android keeps never include photos, to keep them small.</p>

  <h3 id="export">Exporting data</h3>
  <p><strong>Export workout data</strong> generates a CSV file with one row per logged set, including date, exercise name, weight, reps, duration, warmup flag, RPE, RIR, and more. The file is shared via the system share sheet.</p>
  <p>Every set in the database is exported, including ones you never performed. A workout started from a routine creates all of its sets up front, so a session you began and walked out of still has rows.</p>
  <p>Use the <code>is_complete</code> and <code>is_skipped</code> columns to tell them apart: filter on <code>is_complete</code> for anything that should count as training. If you are handing the file to an AI, <a href={`/guides/ai/`}>Use your own AI</a> has a prompt that already explains this.</p>

  <h3 id="ai-context">Copying your context for an AI</h3>
  <p><strong>Copy context for AI</strong> assembles a short plain-text briefing on what you are training. It gathers your <a href={`#training-notes`}>training notes</a>, your active program with its current targets, your last ten sessions, your records, the equipment at your resolved gym, and anything you have marked as avoided.</p>
  <p>It then adds instructions telling the AI how to read the briefing, including how to write a routine back as a <a href={`/badger-format/`}><code>.badger</code> file</a>.</p>
  <p>Badger shows you the whole thing before anything leaves. Read it, then tap <strong>Copy</strong> to put it on your clipboard or <strong>Share</strong> to send it through the system share sheet. <strong>Badger transmits nothing.</strong> The text is assembled on your device, there is no account and no server involved, and where it goes next is entirely your choice.</p>
  <p>The briefing is trimmed to fit a chat window: if you train a lot, older sessions drop first, then equipment, then records. Your notes are the last thing cut, and your constraints and the closing instructions are never dropped. An empty section is left out instead of shown blank.</p>
  <p>Your <strong>body measurements and any health data are deliberately not included</strong>, and nothing from your workout comments, per-set comments or exercise notes travels either. The same action is available at the bottom of your Training notes page, labeled <strong>Copy for AI</strong>. See <a href={`/guides/ai/`}>Use your own AI</a> for what to do with it.</p>

  <h3 id="importing">Importing workout history</h3>
  <p>Badger can import your full history from FitNotes, Strong, Hevy, and Stronglifts. Each option appears in the same Your data section:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Import from FitNotes</strong> - Select a <code>.fitnotes</code> backup file.</li>
    <li><strong>Import from Strong</strong> - Select a Strong CSV export. Weight unit is detected automatically from the column header.</li>
    <li><strong>Import from Hevy</strong> - Select a Hevy CSV export. Warmup sets are imported correctly.</li>
    <li><strong>Import from Stronglifts</strong> - Select a Stronglifts CSV export. Weight unit (kg or lbs) is detected automatically. Every logged set is imported as a working set, since the Stronglifts export does not distinguish warmups.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Imported files are capped at 10&nbsp;MB - comfortably larger than any real export, even for many years of training. If you see a "file is too large" message, the file is almost certainly corrupted; try re-exporting it from the source app.</p>
  <p><strong>Avoid importing the same file twice.</strong> The preview warns you when some of the import's dates already have workouts - importing again adds those sets a second time (duplicating them on those days) rather than replacing them.</p>
  <p>See <a href={`/help/getting-started/#importing`}>Getting started → Importing from another app</a> for the full walkthrough.</p>

  <h3>Debug logs</h3>
  <p><strong>Export debug logs</strong> shares a plain-text file containing the app's recent activity log. The log captures the last ~500 lines written during the current session - useful for diagnosing unexpected behavior or reporting bugs to support. It does not contain personal health or workout data.</p>

  <h3>Deleting data</h3>
  <p><strong>Delete all workout history</strong> sits at the very bottom of Settings. It permanently deletes all workouts and sets, and a confirmation dialog is required. This does not delete exercises or routines.</p>
</HelpLayout>
